content:
carla: Hey @micah — getting 401 invalid_api_key only when the request originates from our Web Worker. Regular page fetches with the exact same token succeed. Any ideas?

micah: Can you paste the minimal main-thread + worker code and the exact headers you send? Also which browser/version? Did you try passing the token as a string vs a Headers/Request object?

carla: Repro: 
Main thread:
```js
const headers = new Headers({
  'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}`,
  'Content-Type': 'application/json'
});
worker.postMessage({ headers }); // passing the Headers instance
```
Worker:
```js
self.addEventListener('message', e => {
  const headers = e.data.headers; // expecting Headers
  fetch('https://api.redwood.com/v1/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers,
    body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: 'hello' })
  })
  .then(r => r.json()).then(console.log).catch(console.error);
});
```
Observed: fetch returns 401 {"error":"invalid_api_key"}. Server-side logs show no Authorization header on the incoming request (we log request.headers and Authorization is missing).
When we console.log(e.data.headers) in the worker it prints {}. Repro on Chrome 118 and Firefox Nightly.

micah: That console behavior is a big hint — Headers instances (and Request/Response objects) are not always preserved across postMessage. Can you try sending a plain object or the raw token string instead? Also paste the exact postMessage call you use from the page.

carla: We were doing worker.postMessage({ headers }); because we wanted to reuse the existing Headers. The token itself is a short Bearer token from our auth service.
Quick test I did that fixed it:
```js
// main thread
const headerObj = Object.fromEntries(headers.entries());
worker.postMessage({ headerObj });
// worker
self.addEventListener('message', e => {
  const headers = new Headers(e.data.headerObj);
  fetch(url, { headers, method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({prompt}) });
});
```
When I switched to sending headerObj the worker fetch included Authorization and the API returned 200.

nguyen: Eng note — confirmed: Headers (and other DOM-native request/response types) are not reliably preserved by the structured clone algorithm across threads in some browsers. Passing a Headers instance will often become {} in the worker so your fetch ends up with empty headers. This is a client-side/browser limitation, not an API auth bug.
Workarounds: send the token string directly (worker.postMessage(token)) or serialize headers via Object.fromEntries(headers.entries()) and reconstruct new Headers() inside the worker. Example: 
```js
// recommended pattern
const headerObj = Object.fromEntries(headers.entries());
worker.postMessage({ headerObj }); // small plain object
// inside worker
const headers = new Headers(e.data.headerObj);
fetch(apiUrl, { headers, method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ prompt }) });
```
If you need to pass a full Request, perform the fetch in the main thread or re-create Request inside the worker. We'll add a short note to the docs / troubleshooting guide about this pattern.

carla: Thanks everyone — that workaround works for us. Patch incoming, marking this resolved. :raised_hands:
